#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069770} :activity/id #uuid "94c7fd02-3d1a-53d9-a362-f4b944c65591" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050334 :topic/id #uuid "40c0038d-ae3b-525b-803b-462840adecbb" :topic/math-academy-id 425} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069770 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069996 :tutorial/content "Given a quadratic equation in the form \n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0,\n$$\n\nwe can find its roots using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{\\color{blue}b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this formula, the expression under the square root is called the **discriminant**:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = {\\color{blue}b^2 - 4ac}\n$$\n\nThe value of the discriminant $\\mathcal{D}$ tells us a lot about the solutions of our quadratic equation.\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}>0,$ then we get $2$ distinct real solutions: one with the positive square root and one with the negative square root.\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}=0,$ then we get $1$ real solution because the square root vanishes. We sometimes say that the equation has two equal solutions.\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}<0,$ then we get no real solutions.\n\nLet's illustrate with some examples:\n\n- If $x^2+3x+2=0,$ then we have a positive discriminant $\\mathcal{D}=3^2-4(1)(2) = 1$ and $2$ distinct real solutions:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{3^2-4(1)(2)} }{2(1)} = \\dfrac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{1} }{2} = \\dfrac{-3 \\pm 1}{2} = -2,-1\n$$\n\n- If $x^2+2x+1=0,$ then we have a zero discriminant $\\mathcal{D}=2^2-4(1)(1) = 0$ and $1$ distinct real solution:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{2^2-4(1)(1)} }{2(1)} = \\dfrac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{0} }{2} = \\dfrac{-2 \\pm 0}{2} = -1\n$$\n\n- If $x^2+x+1=0,$ then we have a negative discriminant $\\mathcal{D}=1^2-4(1)(1) = -3$ and no real solutions:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1^2-4(1)(1)} }{2(1)} = \\dfrac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3} }{2}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "79f4f718-3e60-5bd3-8e3b-25b092e91c76" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6630 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b4cec28d-0479-591e-8c9b-bc2853d4190f" :step/math-academy-id 40122 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069771}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069771 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068832 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068433 :question/id #uuid "360239d4-c271-52f3-8d25-ed47e7dd52f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-10610"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87636457-bdd5-53dd-9137-c070a4e12e84" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050465}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069513} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069524} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234445} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532647} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532678}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Discriminant of a Quadratic Equation"} :step/id #uuid "14778f39-1f57-525b-ae31-35dcbc061af2" :step/math-academy-id 40125 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069772}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069772 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068833 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068434 :question/id #uuid "f667bd43-407c-55a9-9821-67a0c1b11a96" :question/math-academy-id "e-10611"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "926a6ba8-ec05-568a-9c13-c9f4d096e89f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069417} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234447} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532667} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549024}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Discriminant to Determine the Number of Solutions to a Quadratic Equation"} :step/id #uuid "975d250d-99af-536a-8f81-90af7bf4ecad" :step/math-academy-id 40128 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069773}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069773 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068834 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068435 :question/id #uuid "5d4530b7-c6df-583f-b98f-8a8f93203295" :question/math-academy-id "e-10612"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "452d826b-4cb3-56e1-989a-b025b5d5c955" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049986}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233301} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532625} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548997}] :knowledge-point/title "Choosing a Parameter to Force a Desired Number of Solutions to a Quadratic Equation"} :step/id #uuid "6a7f97a5-c2ac-50f8-9a3f-b4d8062db921" :step/math-academy-id 40131 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069774}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069774 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069997 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have the quadratic equation \n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the coefficients $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are all *rational numbers,* and\n\n- the discriminant is non-negative, i.e., $\\mathcal D \\geq 0.$\n\nThen, we have the following possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}$ is a *perfect square*, then the equation has two *rational* solutions.\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}$ is *not* a perfect square, then the equation has two *irrational* solutions.\n\nLet's illustrate with some examples.\n\nConsider the equation $x^2-x-6=0.$ Its discriminant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-1)^{2}-4(1)(-6) \\\\  & =1+24 \\\\  & =25 \\\\  & ={5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a perfect square. Consequently, the corresponding solutions are both rational numbers:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{5^2}}{2(1)} = 3, -2\n$$\n\nNow, consider the equation $x^2-x-5=0.$ Its discriminant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-1)^{2}-4(1)(-5) \\\\  & =1+20 \\\\  & =21,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a perfect square. Consequently, the solutions are both irrational numbers:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{21}}{2}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1fd92ab0-4173-58f8-8b37-79f116479768" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6633 :tutorial/title "Rational Solutions of Quadratic Equations"} :step/id #uuid "2f5a9238-3e51-5658-bd57-7ad3172c3e89" :step/math-academy-id 40147 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069775}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069775 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068835 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068436 :question/id #uuid "b92b9a39-e29a-532f-ba2c-f935773d0bf7" :question/math-academy-id "e-10616"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "adc75098-5640-5e81-a0bc-003fa20c090c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049935} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050358}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233532} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532642} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532709}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadratic Equations With Rational Solutions"} :step/id #uuid "9c9562e4-85a3-56b8-b173-0a8cfad0356a" :step/math-academy-id 40148}] :activity/title "The Discriminant of a Quadratic Equation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068958}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549103} :activity/id #uuid "c1dc22aa-5172-525c-b53a-74cc35f15bd2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050334 :topic/id #uuid "40c0038d-ae3b-525b-803b-462840adecbb" :topic/math-academy-id 425} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549103 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233301 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a93fd21-7680-5bf7-9efe-da3243d5c112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548377} {:answer/id #uuid "0d37168c-ebbc-5e47-83a1-6dd49bf9e7b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=\\frac{-45}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548383} {:answer/id #uuid "1e069ecc-14bf-5842-968d-fa633fe8a380" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=\\frac{45}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548412} {:answer/id #uuid "262a1bc5-1b71-5f81-a67b-75d656e1aa36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=\\frac{4}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548430} {:answer/id #uuid "4593e5c0-5b05-597c-9314-7d94176bf405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=\\frac{-4}{45}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548486}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548412} :answer-field/id #uuid "11f583ac-83fa-5556-91cb-0b01935633ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a04dee46-e349-5f31-9089-697114c8639e" :question/math-academy-id "q-12218" :question/problem "Find the values of $k$ for which the quadratic equation $5{x}^{2}+15x+k=0$ has one distinct real solution." :question/worked-solution "The equation has one distinct real solution if its discriminant is zero. So, we need to find the values of $k$ so that the discriminant is zero.\n\nFirst, we calculate the discriminant. We have $a=5,$ $b=15,$ and $c=k,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(15)^{2}-4(5)(k) \\\\  & =225-20k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the values of $k$ such that the discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =0 \\\\ 225-20k & =0 \\\\ 20k & =225 \\\\ k & =\\frac{225}{20} \\\\ k & =\\frac{45}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation will have one distinct real solution if $k=\\frac{45}{4}.$"} :step/id #uuid "5e21b816-beb3-56a2-9b03-c6291942c7f6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549112}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549106 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548997 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f5241c35-4c53-5fa0-9bb4-acb9e908070a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548845}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548845} :answer-field/id #uuid "4aa3d16b-4100-574f-84df-1c8d2de4cb31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3d7da907-2808-5b79-bfed-ca1f9e228ecb" :question/math-academy-id "q-218748" :question/problem "If the quadratic equation $k{x}^{2}+4x+2=0$ has one distinct real solution, then $k=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The equation has one distinct real solution if its discriminant is zero. So, we need to find the values of $k$ so that the discriminant is zero.\n\nFirst, we calculate the discriminant. We have $a=k,$ $b=4,$ and $c=2,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(4)^{2}-4(k)(2) \\\\  & =16-8k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the values of $k$ such that the discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =0 \\\\ 16-8k & =0 \\\\ 8k & =16 \\\\ k & =\\frac{16}{8} \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation will have one distinct real solution if $k=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "6eeafe58-00df-54fd-bf15-a1b13055fb09"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549112 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46794791-4cb5-55d4-bbfa-4d50cbc19e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548488} {:answer/id #uuid "6c8a3e36-af4d-58f6-9536-c324e5353465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548557} {:answer/id #uuid "7d518a4b-9bbd-5ce2-ba1d-3f2676acccd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548589} {:answer/id #uuid "8fa1764f-f613-564e-a751-183e5ddece0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548636} {:answer/id #uuid "d7b129fd-cf43-5f11-9a88-c2df6b3b3d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548775}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548775} :answer-field/id #uuid "355a3e72-0ca4-5416-b98c-d761ee9e21bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7966ce86-501b-56f6-9e0c-8b7da65a0478" :question/math-academy-id "q-83543" :question/problem "Consider the equation $4{x}^{2}+8x-12=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?" :question/worked-solution "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=4,$ $b=8,$ and $c=-12,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(8)^{2}-4(4)(-12) \\\\  & =64+192 \\\\  & =256\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is positive $(D>0),$ the equation has two distinct real solutions."} :step/id #uuid "75aa9ea7-1039-562b-8c94-a84abad863b3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549117}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da17e968-9000-5bf7-b1b8-4ac69644a8cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065848} {:answer/id #uuid "2d340b06-3adc-5e91-95d4-b12fd2b9610b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065849} {:answer/id #uuid "38e47f0b-ad13-5ad5-b4c6-1a6804c2be89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065850} {:answer/id #uuid "514fb001-491c-5ba7-9ad8-39d5fe33cf32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065851} {:answer/id #uuid "64e5ea06-cda8-5514-b0a3-e1b00faea02c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065852}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065849} :answer-field/id #uuid "5361b2fe-0cc4-5239-b815-9e9fc9c7eb4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "555b4713-c6f1-5ebd-b6b9-f73907d00fc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-150890" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation ${x}^{2}+2x-15=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The discriminant of the equation is a perfect square.\n\nII. The equation has only rational solutions.\n\nIII. The equation has only irrational solutions." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=-15.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(2)^{2}-4(1)(-15) \\\\  & =4+60 \\\\  & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd $64={8}^{2}$ is a perfect square.\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. \n\nFirst, notice that our quadratic equation has rational coefficients. And, since the discriminant is a nonzero perfect square, the equation must have two rational solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "812fd524-4a1b-57bd-bd50-8cf2bc0a2512" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549118}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549118 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549024 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f30d992b-a29e-5752-8a9c-afea2119fa9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548836}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548836} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d35e828-7244-5a93-b7ac-e296f8c506d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548937}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8988f053-e75a-5c75-a866-50d1b2af5964" :question/math-academy-id "q-218746" :question/problem "The equation $2{x}^{2}+5x-9=0$ has{{field-1}}\ndistinct real solutions." :question/worked-solution "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=2,$ $b=5,$ and $c=-9,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={5}^{2}-4(2)(-9) \\\\  & =25+72 \\\\  & =97\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is positive $(D>0),$ the equation has $2$ distinct real solutions."} :step/id #uuid "82745d32-719e-5bc8-8560-08d4296cae8c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549106}}] :activity/title "The Discriminant of a Quadratic Equation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548351}]}
